// REAL DATA. Twelve photographs of handwritten order slips, taken on a phone.
//
// The labels are a transcription of what is on the paper, made by reading the
// images. They have been checked once, by one person - which is exactly the
// weakness to declare in a write-up. Two independent transcriptions, with the
// agreement between them reported, is what this needs before it is evidence.
//
// Three slips (s01, s05, s10) have an item corrected in place. The label says
// what the customer ordered, so a struck-out line is ABSENT from the gold:
// s01 drops "Product B", s05 counts Coffee once and not twice, s10 reads the
// product as "Atta" rather than "Wheat Atta". This is the whole point of the
// set - a character recogniser cannot see a strikethrough, so it will read all
// three as live text, and the gold labels are what make that visible as a
// number rather than an anecdote.
//
// Products here are the text as written, not catalogue rows: these slips come
// from six different trades and no single shop stocks all of it. So this set
// measures extraction, and notes.jsonl measures extraction plus resolution.
